"""
Alhambra (Q47476), Granada — the fortified palace city on the Sabika hill and the Generalife, built by code.

Author: Álvaro C. Chulvi (with AI assistance). Licence of the resulting model: CC BY 4.0.
The monument: Nasrid works of the 13th–14th centuries (Muhammad I began the Alcazaba c. 1238; the palaces of
Comares and of the Lions under Yusuf I, d. 1354, and Muhammad V, d. 1391; the Generalife c. 1300–1319, its upper
storey 1494); the Palace of Charles V by Pedro Machuca (designed 1527, died 1550), continued by his son Luis
Machuca (d. 1572) and finished in the 20th century. Public domain.

What is modelled:
- The walls of the whole perimeter (OpenStreetMap way 92528618, 2.18 km) following the terrain, in reddish-ochre
  rammed earth (the «red castle»), 2.6 m thick, with a parapet and Nasrid merlons with pyramidal caps; the 35
  towers and gates that OSM maps (Comares, la Vela, el Homenaje, las Armas, Quebrada, Hueca, del Cubo, de la
  Pólvora, de los Hidalgos, las Damas, el Peinador de la Reina, los Picos, el Cadí, la Cautiva, las Infantas,
  el Cabo de la Carrera, el Agua, Juan de Arce, Baltasar de la Cruz, Siete Suelos, el Capitán, la Bruja, las
  Cabezas, los Abencerrajes, los Carros, Barba…), the Puerta de la Justicia with its horseshoe arch and the
  Puerta del Vino; the inner walls of the Alcazaba and the other wall stretches inside.
- The Alcazaba at the west tip: its walls, the Torre de la Vela with its bell gable, the Plaza de Armas with the
  foundations of the soldiers' quarter, the Jardín de los Adarves.
- The Nasrid Palaces at one floor level: Mexuar, Cuarto Dorado, the Comares palace with the Court of the
  Myrtles (white marble floor, the long pool, the myrtle hedges and the porticoes of seven arches at both ends),
  the Sala de la Barca and the Comares tower; the Palace of the Lions with its court, its galleries of 124
  slender columns, the two pavilions on the short sides, the fountain with its twelve lions and the four
  channels, the halls around it (Reyes, Mocárabes, Dos Hermanas and Abencerrajes with their lanterns), the
  Baños Reales, the Mirador de Daraxa and the Lindaraja garden; tiled hipped roofs everywhere.
- The Palace of Charles V: the 63 m square, two orders (rusticated Tuscan below with the round windows, Ionic
  above with pedimented windows), the marble portals of the west and south fronts, the circular courtyard with
  32 Doric columns below and 32 Ionic above, and its tiled roof.
- The Partal: the portico of five arches with the Torre de las Damas over its pool, the Oratorio, the terraced
  gardens; the medina: Calle Real with its houses, the church of Santa María, the Baño de la Mezquita, the
  Parador (old convent of San Francisco) and their gardens, the ruins of the medina (Palacio del Mufti, Secano…)
  as low walls.
- The Generalife across the ravine: its palace around the Patio de la Acequia (the long channel with the arcs of
  its jets, the flower beds, the north pavilion with the Sala Regia and the mirador tower, the porticoes), the
  Patio del Ciprés de la Sultana with its pool, the upper and lower gardens with their hedges and cypress rows.
- Gardens, hedges, pools and fountains as OpenStreetMap draws them; the cypress rows of OSM.
- The open ground (since 2026-11-28): everything inside the walls and the Generalife that the model did not cover
  (the Plaza de los Aljibes, the Secano, the yards of the medina, the paths) gets a floor that follows the ground,
  coloured from the IGN orthophoto (PNOA, CC BY 4.0, scne.es; model_sources/Q47476_pnoa.jpg at 1 m/px, its
  shadows softened), and the trees of the gardens and the open ground stand where the orthophoto shows their
  crowns (dark green blobs), not at random. Before, the game saw through those holes and drew its own ground.
- Not modelled: the terrain itself, the streets, the woods outside the walls (Bosque de la Alhambra), the
  interiors and their stucco, the Hotel Alhambra Palace and other buildings outside the walls.

Measurements (see meta.json for the sources):
- Plan: OpenStreetMap — the walls way 92528618 (barrier=city_wall, wikidata Q47476), towers and gates (35 ways),
  the Alcazaba 26935518, the Nasrid palaces 279110939 and their halls, the Palace of Charles V relation 2640220
  (outer square 62.8 × 61.6 m, circular courtyard Ø 30.8 m), the Generalife 196490785 and its palace relation
  14361006, courts, pools, gardens, hedges and tree rows (ids in DATA). Data © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL.
- Ground: since 2026-11-28 the IGN MDT05 (bare earth, 5 m; CC BY 4.0, scne.es) as resampled at 8 m in
  models/Q47476/terrain.json (tools/terrain.py) — the same ground Offlined puts around the model, so model and
  terrain meet. Before, EU-DEM 25 m (Copernicus, via OpenTopoData, sampled on a 12 m grid: DATA terrain, still
  the fallback without terrain.json), which differed from the IGN ground by up to ±10 m.
  y = 0 is the Plaza de los Aljibes (773.7 m in the IGN MDT05; 764 m in the old EU-DEM); the levels below are
  taken from that ground: the Nasrid palaces at y = −4.5, the Plaza de Armas of the Alcazaba at +4.5 (the old
  EU-DEM, smoothing the spur, gave −10), the Partal pool at −3.3, the Patio de la Acequia of the Generalife at
  +24.3; the ground at the foot of the walls goes down to about −25 (north front, over the Darro).
- Walls: walk 9.5 m above the outside ground or 3 m above the inside ground, whichever is higher (smoothed over
  ~25 m), parapet 0.5 m, merlons 1.15 m + 0.35 m cap (photos, ±15 %); foundations 6 m below the lowest ground.
  Wikipedia (es): the site is about 740 m long and 40–180 m wide (OSM gives 744 × 262 m for the smallest
  rectangle around the walls). Towers rise 3.5 m above the walk (tower-palaces 5 m), estimated from photos.
- Torre de Comares: 45 m from its foot on the Darro side (alhambra.info, ecured); modelled as a terrace 25 m above
  the court of the Myrtles (Hall of the Ambassadors 11.3 m square, 18.2 m high inside, en.wikipedia), merlons above,
  i.e. 37 m above the smoothed DEM foot and 44 m above the bottom of its foundations.
  Torre de la Vela: 25 m above its outer foot (en.wikipedia), 20 m above the Plaza de Armas; Torre del Homenaje:
  26 m (en.wikipedia; 22–26 m es.wikipedia), 18 m above the plaza.
- Court of the Myrtles 36.6 × 23.5 m, pool 34 × 7.1 m (en.wikipedia; OSM agrees within 1 m); Court of the Lions
  28.7 × 15.6 m with 124 marble columns and twelve lions (en/es.wikipedia), the open court and its galleries taken
  from OSM ways 1484408898/902/904/905; galleries 3 m deep, columns 2.7 m to the capital (photos, ±15 %).
- Palace of Charles V: 63 m square, 17 m high (en.wikipedia), courtyard Ø 30.8 m (OSM), 32 + 32 columns.
- Generalife: Patio de la Acequia 48.7 × 12.8 m (en.wikipedia), mirador 3.98 m square; one storey 7.5 m and the
  north pavilion 11.5 m with the mirador tower to 15.5 m (photos, ±15 %).
- Other buildings: OSM building:levels × 3.4 m; Nasrid halls estimated from photos (±20 %).

Axes (docs/MODEL_SPEC.md): +Y up, the north front over the Darro valley — the view from the Mirador de San
Nicolás, with the Comares tower in the middle — faces +Z (heading 26°, square to the long axis of the walls,
which runs at 116°/296°); +X points to 296° (west-north-west, the Alcazaba). Origin on the ground at the centroid
of the walled area (way 92528618).

    python model_sources/Q47476_alhambra.py   -> models/Q47476/model.glb (+ footprint in meta.json)
"""
